acks on  to the surface helps promote the  polymerization quickly so that the the  droplet or the dome of Maitre gel in  which you resuspend it selves you know  polymerizes quickly so that it doesn't  lose its shape and basically just become  trapped in what looks like a coating on  your plate and so this is commonly used  for growing certain cell lines as well  as patient-derived cells and various  types of adult stem cell organized  models  and just by way of example here so this  picture on the top left is an example of  three domes of an extracellular matrix  mimic in which cells have been  reconstituted so that they can grow into  steroids other multiple spheroids or one  spheroid depending on the model system  and what the cells were like before you  resuspended them in the matrix one other  thing that that's important to point out  is that these materials are also quite  viscous and so in order to pipette  accurate accurately with these materials  it's definitely a good idea to work with  positive displacement pipette instead of  the air displacement pipettes which are  probably a little more standard in most  cell biology or molecular biology labs  it's shirts certainly plays dividends in  pipetting the exact same volume from  from droplets to droplet which can  impact how how these things are being  fed so adult stem cells are grown in  this way where cells isolated from  tissue are re suspended or reconstituted  reconstituted inside of an exerciser  matrix and many of them grow into what  almost looks like bubbles inside the gel  so they form these cystic like  structures but if you actually stain  them with something such as Daffy you  can see that there is these cellular  [Music]  models these are lighting up all the  different nuclei representing all the  different cells in a given model shown  here on the right but this is this  really only works if you're growing  cells inside of an ECM another way in  which ECM is used which is maybe a  little more common or commonly seen in  the pluripotent stem cell space and that  is to either to take aggregates or  previously formed aggregates of cells  and to put them inside droplets of of  Maitre gel or gel tracks and so the red  arrow here and the picture on the bottom  is actually showing a spheroid of a  growing organizer should say that is  differentiating into  a certain lineages and at a certain time  point we have to work very quickly and  keeping the temperature sensitivity of  the material in mind try dispense or  deposit spheroids in sided droplets and  position them so that when the gel  phlegm arises there they're fully  encased so that they can then go on to  grow into whatever model system you're  looking at the picture shown here on the  bottom right is just a picture of a of a  neural organoid and you can kind of see  around the edges here a lot of the  almost like folding full of epithelial  egg structures although the center is  pretty dark suggesting that the center  probably will have a lot of dead cells  and this is not to on too uncommon and  certainly something to keep an eye on  when you're monitoring B the health of  your system  so as I wind down here in the last  couple of slides a couple the other  things that are probably relevant or  important for for people who are looking  to get started in 3d or who are only  recently started in 3d is that these  model systems tends to take very long to  grow so some of the cell line model  systems are probably much shorter they  would they will grow on the order of  days to maybe a week or two depending on  what model system you're using and how  large you're trying to have it grow too  but typically in the organoid space  protocols lasts anywhere from several  weeks potentially to many months before  they're actually sort of at an end point  where they're useful for a say our  potentially useful for a say and so this  is done to help promote the maturation  of different cell types especially if  you're talking with pluripotent stem  cells but this the take home really is  that because these are really long  protocols it's really important to plan  ahead to make sure you have everything  you need to sustain you your culture  through the term of the of the  experiment so that you you don't sort of  go oh I've been culturing for a month  I've got one more month to go and you  find out you're in-between orders and  and suddenly you might not have material  for the next media change although that  that sounds a little bit pandering it is  really not meant to it's just meant to  emphasize that in these line protocols  I mean planning as always is really  important but they're really thinking  planning maybe a little longer term than  then one may be a little more customed  to doing another part of this also has  to do with what we call it earlier the  less common culture formats the use of  you know roller bottles or mini  bioreactors or spinner flasks or the use  of orbital shakers maybe not always in  the form of flats like shown here  sometimes their orbital shakers that you  would put in your incubator on which you  know it's a flat platform so you can  like various types of dishes in there  for agitation to help with the feeding  and long-term growth of your organized  muddled system so the reason why this is  certainly something to plan for is  because these things take up different  amounts of space in in a typical cell  culture incubator then you're then  simply putting plates on on a shelf will  typically do so the space required to  grow these is certainly something  different that that definitely requires  planning for especially if this is not  the only thing your lab is doing if you  want to maintain space for other  experiments that maybe aren't using  these sorts of less common culture  formats you know it does require  planning ahead and of course working  with your colleagues the other important  feature to remember here with with  respect to the length 